<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.8.2: Average Rate of Change and Slope</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00982</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.8.2: Average Rate of Change and Slope</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e13d400a-70a3-4361-a8ac-4a98bf3a7d5e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">The table and graph show a more complete picture of the temperature
        changes on the same winter day. The function <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>T</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> gives the temperature, in degrees
        Fahrenheit, <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>h</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> hours since noon.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">

        <media alt="A scatter plot showing temperature in °F versus hours since noon. Points start at 20°F at 0 hours, peak at 25°F at 4 hours, then drop to 8°F at 10 hours. There is a data point for every hour since noon.">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_11_03_01.svg"/>
        </media>

      </para>
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="14">
          <tbody align="center">
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry> 0 </entry>
              <entry> 1 </entry>
              <entry> 2 </entry>
              <entry> 3 </entry>
              <entry> 4 </entry>
              <entry> 5 </entry>
              <entry> 6 </entry>
              <entry> 7 </entry>
              <entry> 8 </entry>
              <entry> 9 </entry>
              <entry> 10 </entry>
              <entry> 11 </entry>
              <entry> 12 </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mi>T</m:mi>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                      <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry> 18 </entry>
              <entry> 19 </entry>
              <entry> 20 </entry>
              <entry> 20 </entry>
              <entry> 25 </entry>
              <entry> 23 </entry>
              <entry> 17 </entry>
              <entry> 15 </entry>
              <entry> 11 </entry>
              <entry> 11 </entry>
              <entry> 8 </entry>
              <entry> 6 </entry>
              <entry> 7 </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      <para id="para-00003">In questions 1 – 3, find the average rate of change for the following
        intervals. Be prepared to show your reasoning. </para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">between noon and 1 p.m.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00007">1°F per hour. The temperature rose by 1°F in 1 hour.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">between noon and 4 p.m.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">1.75°F per hour. The temperature increased by 7°F in 4 hours. The
            average increase is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            or 1.75.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">between noon and midnight</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00015">–0.92°F an hour. I found the slope of the line connecting the two
            points, and it is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>11</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            .</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00016">In questions 4 – 6, use the slope formula to find the slope of the line
        that connects the following points. </para>
      <!--Q4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00017"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>19</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>19</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q5-->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00021"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>25</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00009"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>25</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00025"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00013"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>11</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00029">Compare your answers to questions 1 – 3 with your answers to
            questions 4 – 6. Explain what this tells you about the average rate of change and the
            slope of a line.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00032">The average rate of change between two points is the same as the
            slope of the line connecting the 2 points.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00033">For questions 8 and 9, think back to Mai and Tyler’s disagreement about
        temperature to solve these problems. </para>
      <!--Q8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00034">Use the <term id="term-00001">average rate of change</term> to show
            which time period—4 p.m. to 6 p.m. or 6 p.m. to 10 p.m.—experienced a faster temperature
            drop.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00036">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00037">The temperature dropped faster between 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. For
            example: In the first interval, the temperature fell 8°F in 2 hours, which is an average
            drop of 4°F an hour. In the second, it fell 9°F in 4 hours, which is an average drop of
            2.25°F per hour.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q9-->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00038">Use the slope formula to show which line has the greater slope—the
            line connecting the points that represent the temperature at 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. or the
            line connecting the points that represent the temperature at 6 p.m. and 10 p.m.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00041">The slope of the line representing the temperature between 4 p.m.
            and 6 p.m. has a greater slope. The slope of the line connecting the points representing
            the temperature between 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>17</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>25</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. The slope of the line
            connecting the points representing the temperature between 6 p.m. and 10 p.m. is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>17</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2.25</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q10-->
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00042">Compare your answers to question 8 and question 9.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00044">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00045">Finding the average rate of change and using the slope formula give
            the same results. The average rate of change and the slope of the line between hours 4
            and 6 is greater than the average rate of change and the slope of the line between hours
            6 and 10.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <!--Interaction
    End -->
    <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
    will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->

    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00003">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>

        <!--Start
        interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
        have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
        button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00011">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00046">Refer to graph and table of the previous function <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>T</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> to answer the
              questions:</para>
            <para id="para-00047"> Over what interval did the temperature decrease the most rapidly?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00048">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00049">between 5 p.m. and 6 p.m.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
        interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00012">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00050">Over what interval did the temperature increase the most rapidly?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00052">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00053">between 3 p.m. and 4 p.m.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
        interaction-->
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00014">
        <problem id="prob-00014">
        <para id="para-00018"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_4_8_2_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>

      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Finding Rates of Change</title>
        <para id="para-00056">Jay was racing bikes with a friend on the way to the park. A table and
          a graph of the times are shown below.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00003">
          <tgroup cols="2">
            <tbody align="center">
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>Time (minutes)</emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>Distance (miles)</emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 0 </entry>
                <entry> 0 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 5 </entry>
                <entry> 0.84 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 10 </entry>
                <entry> 1.86 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 15 </entry>
                <entry> 3.00 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 20 </entry>
                <entry> 4.27 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 25 </entry>
                <entry> 5.67 </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
          <caption>Jay</caption>
        </table>
        <para id="para-00057">
          <media alt="A scatterplot that shows the relationship between time in minutes (x-axis) and distance in miles (y-axis) plotted points include (0, 0), (5, 0.84), (10, 1..86), (15, 3), (20, 42.7), and (25, 5.67).">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_11_05_01.jpg"/>
          </media>

        </para>
        <para id="para-00058">What is the average rate of change of the first 10 minutes of the
          race?</para>
        <para id="para-00059">To find the average rate of change of the first 10 minutes of the
          race, you would look at the first data value on the table and the third data value on the
          table, since it corresponds to 10 minutes.</para>
        <para id="para-00060">Remember, the average rate of change formula is <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>,
          where <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> typically
          represents your second data point and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> represents
          your first data point.</para>
        <para id="para-00061">For this situation, it would be <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> or <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>10</m:mn>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1.86</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> or <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>10</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1.86</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00062">Substituting the points into the average rate of change formula
          results in the following:</para>
        <para id="para-00063"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>,
          which is <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>1.86</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>
          or 0.186 miles per minute.</para>
        <para id="para-00064">Notice that the answer is positive and the points on the graph are
          higher each time.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Finding Rates of Change</title>
      <para id="para-00065">Jay was racing bikes
          with a friend on the way to the park. A table and a graph of the times are shown below.</para>
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00004">
            <tgroup cols="2">
              <tbody align="center">
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Time (minutes)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Distance (miles)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 0 </entry>
                  <entry> 0 </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 5 </entry>
                  <entry> 0.84 </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 10 </entry>
                  <entry> 1.86 </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 15 </entry>
                  <entry> 3.00 </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 20 </entry>
                  <entry> 4.27 </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 25 </entry>
                  <entry> 5.67 </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
            <caption>Jay</caption>
          </table>
      <para id="para-00066">
            <media alt="A scatterplot that shows the relationship between time in minutes (x-axis) and distance in miles (y-axis) plotted points include (0, 0), (5, 0.84), (10, 1..86), (15, 3), (20, 42.7), and (25, 5.67).">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_11_05_02.jpg"/>
            </media>

          </para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00013">
            <problem id="prob-00013">
              <para id="para-00067">What was Jay’s average rate of change from the beginning to 25
          minutes later?</para>
              <para id="para-00068">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
          button to compare your work. </para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00013">
              <para id="para-00069">Compare your answer:</para>
              <para id="para-00070">Here is how to find the average rate of change of the first 25
          minutes of the race:</para>
              <para id="para-00071">Remember, the average rate of change formula is <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                        <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>,
          where <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          typically represents your second data point and <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          represents your first data point.</para>
              <para id="para-00072">For this situation, it would be <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          or <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>25</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>5.67</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          or <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>25</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>5.67</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>.</para>
              <para id="para-00073">Substituting the points into the average rate of change formula
          results in the following:</para>
              <para id="para-00074"><m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                        <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>5.67</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>25</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>,
          which is 0.227 miles per minute.</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>

  </content>
</document>
